        </li>
      ))}
    </ol>
  </nav>
  <header class="id-header">
    <p class="id-pillar">Lore · World · MYW·09</p>
    <h1>Ravens in Heraldry</h1>
    <p class="id-subtitle">From the raven banner to coats of arms</p>
  </header>

  <section class="id-snippet" aria-label="Quick answer">
    <p><strong class="claim-lore">[lore]</strong> Ravens appear in heraldry across Northern and Western Europe — most prominently in the Norse <strong>raven banner</strong> (<em>hrafnsmerki</em>), a war standard associated with Odin's favour, and in later formal heraldry in Welsh, Irish, and Cornish family arms. As a heraldic charge, the raven typically represents martial prowess, Norse ancestry, or territorial association with raven-named places.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="id-section">
    <h2>The Viking Raven Banner</h2>
    <p><strong class="claim-lore">[lore]</strong> The <em>hrafnsmerki</em> (raven mark/banner) was a Norse war standard. Historical and saga sources attest it:</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Anglo-Saxon Chronicle</strong> (878 CE): references a "raven banner" (<em>hræfn</em>) captured from Danish forces at Cynwit; states the banner was woven by daughters of Ragnar Lothbrok and had magical properties — if the raven appeared to flutter, victory was at hand; if it drooped, the army would be defeated</li>
      <li><strong>Annals of St Neots</strong> (later interpolation): gives a similar account</li>
      <li>Saga references: raven banners appear in Njáls saga and other Icelandic sagas as prestige war standards</li>
    </ul>
    <p>The raven banner combined the raven's association with Odin and battlefield victory into a physical standard. Its magical framing (the raven "moving" in wind as a divine omen) aligns with Norse traditions about divine favour in battle.<sup><a href="#src-1">1</a>,<a href="#src-2">2</a></sup></p>
  </section>

  <section class="id-section">
    <h2>The Raven in Formal Heraldry</h2>
    <p><strong class="claim-lore">[lore]</strong> In the heraldic system that developed from the 12th century onward in Western Europe, ravens appear as charges in arms for several reasons:</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Norse ancestry claims:</strong> Families with claimed Norse or Scandinavian descent used raven charges as an allusion to Odin and the raven banner</li>
      <li><strong>Canting arms (punning):</strong> Families whose names derive from words for raven — <em>bran</em> (Welsh: crow/raven), <em>corby</em> (Norman French: crow/raven from Lat. <em>corvus</em>) — used ravens in their arms as a visual pun on the name</li>
      <li><strong>Territorial association:</strong> Places named after ravens (many Old Norse or Old English place names contain <em>hrafn</em> or <em>hræfn</em>) generated arms with raven charges for the families associated with those places</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section class="id-section">
    <h2>Notable Examples</h2>
    <p><strong class="claim-lore">[lore]</strong></p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Isle of Man:</strong> Three legs of Man (triskelion) is the primary symbol, but Manx heraldry also incorporates raven imagery from the island's Norse history</li>
      <li><strong>Corbet / Corby families:</strong> English and Scottish families with Norman French <em>corbet</em> (crow/raven) names bore ravens in their arms — a classic canting arms example</li>
      <li><strong>Welsh families:</strong> Some Welsh armorial bearings derive from the Brân Fendigaid tradition (Brân = raven/crow in Welsh); Arthurian raven associations in Welsh heraldic tradition</li>
      <li><strong>Danish and Swedish heraldry:</strong> Raven charges appear in arms of families with explicit Norse ancestry claims</li>
    </ul>
    <p>The raven is a "sable" (black) charge in heraldry — the colour associated with grief and constancy, adding to its symbolic resonance in a tradition where colour carries meaning.<sup><a href="#src-3">3</a></sup></p>
  </section>

  <section class="id-section">
    <h2>Raven vs Crow in Heraldry</h2>
    <p><strong class="claim-lore">[lore]</strong> Heraldic blazons do not always distinguish ravens from crows — both appear as generic large black birds (<em>corbie</em> or <em>corbeau</em> in Norman French heraldry). The term <em>corbie</em> specifically in Scottish heraldry refers to a crow or raven. Heralds describe the bird's posture (<em>passant</em>, <em>displayed</em>, <em>close</em>) rather than its species in most cases. Identification of a heraldic charge as specifically a raven (vs crow) depends on context and blazon language.<sup><a href="#src-3">3</a></sup></p>
  </section>

  <section class="id-faq id-section" aria-labelledby="faq-h2">
